[
    {
        "content": "<p>We currently have <code>simp</code> lemmas that simplify <code>a ⊔ b</code>/<code>a ⊓ b</code> given that <code>a ≤ b</code> or <code>b ≤ a</code>. This is useful, but it does mean that simp gets a lot of recursive calls, which slows <code>simp</code> down:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">set_option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trace</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">profiler</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Lattice</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">j</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">j</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- takes over 0.5s</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">j</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">j</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">sup_of_le_left</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">sup_of_le_right</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- this is instant</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The reason why I'm bringing this up is that <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42316\">#42316</a>, which unifies the underlying constant of <code>∪</code>/<code>∩</code> for sets with <code>⊔</code>/<code>⊓</code>. It causes a 0.2% slowdown in mathlib because <code>simp</code> got more powerful by having these simp lemmas for <code>⊔</code>/<code>⊓</code> now also apply to sets.</p>\n<p>To fix this, I made a PR removing the bad <code>@[simp]</code> attributes (<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43436\">#43436</a>). It speeds up mathlib by 0.12%.</p>\n<p>So I would like to ask, is the convenience of having these <code>@[simp]</code> lemmas worth the ~0.3% slowdown, or not?</p>",
        "id": 623839434,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1789286548
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>My vote is \"not\". There's no way a single (or pair) simp lemma should have such a large negative effect on Mathlib.</p>",
        "id": 623874629,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1789308688
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The simp tag is only useful if simp can discharge the a \\le b condition, right? And proving inequalities isn’t simp’s forte.</p>",
        "id": 623874938,
        "sender_full_name": "David Loeffler",
        "timestamp": 1789308920
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Arguably, the problem is <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=PartialOrder.mkOfGroupCone_le_iff#doc\">docs#PartialOrder.mkOfGroupCone_le_iff</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=PartialOrder.mkOfAddGroupCone_le_iff#doc\">docs#PartialOrder.mkOfAddGroupCone_le_iff</a> (and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=LowerAdjoint.closure_le_closed_iff_le#doc\">docs#LowerAdjoint.closure_le_closed_iff_le</a> to a lesser degree). These two <code>simp</code> lemmas cause uncacheable instance synthesis that is guaranteed to fail and is repeated on every occurrence of <code>_ &lt;= _</code>. Without them, the <code>simp</code> call takes just about 0.1 seconds.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, all three lemmas are only used once in mathlib, so it seems safe to just remove the <code>simp</code> attribute.</p>",
        "id": 623897896,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1789324110
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In what sense are the instance syntheses uncacheable?</p>",
        "id": 623899209,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1789324986
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>They have metavariables</p>",
        "id": 623899232,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1789325002
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The call looks like this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Meta</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">synthInstance</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">💥️</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SetLike</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?</span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 623899284,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1789325036
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Alright, <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43778\">#43778</a> has a 0.05% speedup but perhaps this also further decreases the impact of <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42316\">#42316</a>?</p>",
        "id": 623901381,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1789326440
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That's nice! We can merge that and then see how if affects the performance of my two PRs.</p>",
        "id": 623901826,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1789326758
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oops, I think that is my fault. Why are those <code>simp</code> lemmas bad?</p>",
        "id": 623907736,
        "sender_full_name": "Artie Khovanov",
        "timestamp": 1789330303
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh I see, <code>mkOfGroupCone</code> is reducible so <code>simp</code> sees through it and tries on every <code>LE.le</code>?</p>",
        "id": 623907871,
        "sender_full_name": "Artie Khovanov",
        "timestamp": 1789330433
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well the problem is that the discrimination tree doesn't match instances (or more precisely, class-typed values), so even if the lemmas are only about <code>mkOfGroupCone</code> and are only useful if the instance is literally <code>mkOfGroupCone</code>, this can't be matched in the discrimination tree so it just gets the very general keys <code>@LE.le _ _ _ _</code>.</p>",
        "id": 623907980,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1789330517
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And defeq will then try to synthesize the prerequisite class <code>[SetLike S G]</code> and fails; but not without traversing the discrimination tree for <code>SetLike</code> to find <em>every</em> instance of <code>SetLike</code>.</p>",
        "id": 623908147,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1789330622
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"873547\">@Robin Arnez</span> care to write a linter for such bad <code>simp</code> lemmas? Even if it only uses heuristics to narrow down which ones are \"actually\" bad so that there aren't so many false positives, that might be quite helpful. Although I expect that perhaps some the new <code>IsFooApply</code> type classes might have the same issue.</p>",
        "id": 623941941,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1789353076
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I really like the simp lemmas Jovan is unsimping in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43436\">#43436</a>. Could we make sure that they are still expensive after  removing the bad simp lemmas Robin found?</p>",
        "id": 624012634,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1789379393
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"873547\">Robin Arnez</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Should.20.60max.20a.20b.60.20simplify.20using.20.60a.20.E2.89.A4.20b.60.3F/near/623907980\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Well the problem is that the discrimination tree doesn't match instances (or more precisely, class-typed values), so even if the lemmas are only about <code>mkOfGroupCone</code> and are only useful if the instance is literally <code>mkOfGroupCone</code>, this can't be matched in the discrimination tree so it just gets the very general keys <code>@LE.le _ _ _ _</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Hm, then I don't understand why <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"310045\">@Eric Wieser</span> is saying in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43778\">#43778</a> that changing the abbrev to a semireducible def solves it?</p>",
        "id": 624013612,
        "sender_full_name": "Artie Khovanov",
        "timestamp": 1789379660
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yeah I'm also not sure what he meant by that; even if it would be semireducible, it would still have bad keys</p>",
        "id": 624015767,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1789380230
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hmm, I hadn't realized that class-typed values were really excluded fully from trees</p>",
        "id": 624016066,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1789380308
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"387244\">Yaël Dillies</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Should.20.60max.20a.20b.60.20simplify.20using.20.60a.20.E2.89.A4.20b.60.3F/near/624012634\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Could we make sure that they are still expensive after  removing the bad simp lemmas Robin found?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now, <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43436\">#43436</a> gives a speedup of only 0.06%, but <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42316\">#42316</a> still gives a slowdown of <del>0.21%</del> 0.19%. So, the combined cost of these lemmas will be about <del>0.27%</del> 0.25% after <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42316\">#42316</a>.</p>",
        "id": 624163851,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1789415262
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You sure you've merged it into <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42316\">#42316</a>? I don't see it there</p>",
        "id": 624164655,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1789415511
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ah, I might not have, let me try again.</p>",
        "id": 624164949,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1789415594
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ok, it did help a bit: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42316\">#42316</a> now gives a slowdown of 0.19%.</p>",
        "id": 624171174,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1789416867
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Right; and how does the impact change when we stack <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43436\">#43436</a> onto <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42316\">#42316</a>?</p>",
        "id": 624171981,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1789417113
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I guess the question is; does the slowdown entirely go away by merging <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43436\">#43436</a> or does it only reduce it to around 0.13%</p>",
        "id": 624172215,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1789417174
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Doing this stack will be some work, because now part of the changes will need to be reverted. To give an idea though, I investigated and fixed the files that slowed down the most in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42316\">#42316</a>, and all of them were directly caused by these simp lemmas. So I think that most if not all of the slowdown does come from these simp lemmas.</p>",
        "id": 624206938,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1789431026
    }
]